[Music]  even though the pandemic isn't  completely behind us travel is expected  to continue surging into the new year  the tsa says that air travel is up by  nearly double from a year ago regional  airlines say they're already  experiencing a pilot shortage and that  could affect the major airlines since  they hire from the regionals we have  almost 100 airplanes effectively  grounded right now we have to have a  higher supply of pilots and we just  don't have enough today and all of us  travelers could potentially feel the  effects by seeing fewer flight options  and even higher ticket prices  now all of these ripple effects have  brought a controversial 2013 airline  regulation back into focus it's called  the 1500 hour rule so what is it and why  is it relevant today i had to jump back  more than a decade to find the answers  [Applause]  on an ice cold night the flaming  wreckage of continental flight 3407. in  2009 a regional plane stalled in the air  and then crashed into a house killing 50  people outside of buffalo new york  investigators later found that the  captain's poor training was at fault  congress began searching for solutions  to prevent future pilot errors we do  need to make sure there's a standard of  safety  for every american and one of its  proposed reforms was the 1500 hour rule  the nickname came from the fact that it  would require aspiring pilots in the u.s  including co-pilots to have 1500 hours  of prior flight experience before taking  the controls of a passenger or cargo  plane that is a six-fold increase from  the previous minimum of 250 hours as wsj  reported at the time airline officials  warned that the rule would increase  costs for pilot training which would  aggravate the already dwindling supply  of commercial aviators leaving smaller  airlines understaffed and smaller cities  without essential air services but  congress was undeterred we've got to fix  these problems and it's going to take  both more hours and better quality and i  support any efforts that will get us  there at the order of congress the  federal aviation administration  implemented the 1500 hour rule in 2013.  even though there are some exceptions to  the 1500 hour rule for pilots with  certain kinds of degrees or training the  requirements are still high compared to  other parts of the world for perspective  pilots in europe and canada only have to  have two or three hundred hours before  they step into the cockpit so what  happened next as expected the rule added  years and tens of thousands of dollars  to the process of becoming a pilot in  the u.s and just months after it went  into effect airlines in the u.s faced a  pilot shortage leading some to cut  routes to smaller airports and even file  for bankruptcy the shortage led airlines  to pay pilots higher salaries and ramp  up partnerships with flight schools and  universities to improve the pathways to  the profession to this day there's a  dispute in the industry over whether the  rule has improved safety as you can  imagine the 1500 hour rule has remained  a sensitive point of contention when  republicans led an effort to revise it  in 2017 to expand service to smaller  airports democrats quickly shut down the  idea we're never going to give up  never as you never will give up  washington left the rule as is and  airlines scrambled to get new candidates  in the pipeline  then came the pandemic which presented  unprecedented challenges for the airline  industry when covet hit people stopped  traveling altogether and airlines were  incentivized to offer pilots early  retirements that's because in order to  take advantage of government aid through  the cares act companies weren't allowed  to furlough workers at the four biggest  u.s airlines more than 4 400 pilots  opted to depart last year and when  vaccines arrived we all rushed to snap  up our tickets as travel takes off again  airlines are scrambling to keep pace  with the summer surge after crunching  the numbers consulting firm oliver wyman  says that the u.s could face a shortage  of 7 000 pilots by 2025. to understand  how this will impact airlines and all of  us travelers i called a partner at that  consulting firm jeff murray who happens  to be a former pilot himself and if  you're retired you can't come back and  get your seniority and your route is  that right that is absolutely right so  the the airline pilot profession is a  seniority based profession they would  have to come back as a junior pilot that  means a co-pilot a first officer working  weekends earning first officer pay yeah  forget it exactly no way they're going  to come back exactly so it was a really  unique challenge that um that the  charisma put in place as you can imagine  airlines need a huge amount of pilots  and they've embarked on a hiring spree  the big u.s carriers delta southwest  united and american are on track to hire  13 000 pilots this year and next but  here's the thing the majors frequently  hire from the regionals these are  companies whose official names are  endeavor air and psa airlines but that  commonly fly under names like delta  connection and american eagle and the  regionals say they're already  experiencing a pilot shortage they're  the first point of pain with respect to  the pilot shortage so they will feel it  first this is what a regional airline  executive recently said on an earnings  call the shortages of pilots driven by  the federally mandated 1500 hour rule  and now exacerbated by early retirements  that the major carriers will require our  largest focus over the near term and if  airlines cannot get the pilots they need  how will travelers how will all of us  consumers feel the effects so we're  going to see it through reduced  schedules so you're still going to be  able to get where you want to go but you  likely won't have the flexibility that  you've had historically and you're  probably going to be paying a little  more plus if there's bad weather jeff  says airlines won't be able to recover  as quickly as they usually do since  their staffs aren't as big okay so on to  the big question will the 1500 hour rule  ever change everyone i talked to for  this story supporters and critics say  it's unlikely that it will ever just be  done away with but some airline  executives hope the faa could provide  more exceptions to it like allowing  highly relevant simulator hours to count  toward the 1500 hours and they say  improving government aid options would  help aspiring pilots the problem to  become a pilot particularly at the 1500  hour rule is you need to spend 150 000  and that's typically not eligible for  government loans so creating ways that  they can finance and fund  their ability to get the requisite  training is really the foundation and i  think it's the only way we're going to  solve this problem for small communities  because i expect it's going to get worse  before it gets better when i reached out  to the faa for comment they sent me a  press release about the rule from 2013.  some believe it would take an act of  congress to make changes  so do you think congress and the faa  should revisit the 1500 hour rule drop  me a line in the comments below and if  you liked this story and thought it was  informative please hit subscribe so you  can join me as i cover more news that's  impacting our lives  safe travels

